<h1>Post's correspondence problem</h1>
<p>
(classic problem)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Given a <a href="set.html"><em>set</em></a> of pairs of <a href="string.html"><em>strings</em></a>, find a sequence of pairs such that the concatenation of all first members of the pairs is the same string as the concatenation of all second members. This is an <a href="undecidableProblem.html"><em>undecidable problem</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> PCP.
</p>


<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorSKS">SKS</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong> <a href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Post/" target="_blank">Emil Post</a></strong>, <em>A Variant of a Recursively Unsolvable Problem.</em> Bulletin of the American Mathematical Society, 52:264-268, 1946.
</p>

